<BaseLayout title="About the Witness">
<header class="editorial-hero editorial-hero--about"><div class="kicker">About</div><h1>About the Witness</h1><p>One life. Several ways of making sense of it.</p></header>
<main class="prose reading about-witness">
<p>I have spent a good portion of my life taking things apart.</p>
<p>Machines. Buildings. Business systems. Arguments. Assumptions. Occasionally, my own spectacularly ill-conceived decisions.</p>
<p><strong>Most things went back together better than I found them.</strong> Others taught me why the instructions existed in the first place.</p>
<p>Along the way, I developed an abiding curiosity about the distance between what people say they believe and what they actually do. About the peculiar ways institutions lose their purpose. About the things we inherit without questioning, the things we destroy without understanding, and the things worth preserving even when preservation is inconvenient.</p>
<p>That curiosity eventually needed somewhere to go.</p>
<p><strong>This is where it landed.</strong></p>
<h2>More Than One Way to Tell the Truth</h2>
<p>You'll find essays here. Some examine power, government, civic responsibility, and the machinery of institutions. Others wander into philosophy, spirituality, human behavior, history, and the complicated business of being alive.</p>
<p>You'll also find music.</p>
<p>Under the name <a href="/music/">Bugsy McRae</a>, I write and record songs that explore some of the same territory from another direction. Sometimes a song can get past defenses that an argument only makes stronger. Sometimes three verses and a chorus can say what twenty pages of carefully constructed prose cannot.</p>
<p>There are shorter observations, unfinished questions, occasional provocations, and whatever else earns its place.</p>
<p>I don't see these as separate identities or competing pursuits.</p>
<p>They're different instruments in the same workshop.</p>
<h2>Other Things Growing in the Workshop</h2>
<p>Of course, writing essays and making music haven't entirely satisfied my appetite for taking things apart, questioning their purpose, and occasionally attempting to put them back together in a more interesting arrangement.</p>
<p>There are other projects.</p>
<p>Some are practical. Some are philosophical. Some are experiments in what happens when a perfectly reasonable idea is allowed to wander off without adult supervision.</p>
<p><strong>Backdraft Upcraft</strong> is one of them, an evolving project with its own identity and ambitions. As it develops, you'll find glimpses of that work here.</p>
<p><strong>Common Authority</strong> grew from a conviction that civic responsibility cannot be outsourced indefinitely to institutions, political organizations, or whoever happens to be shouting the loudest. It's an exploration of what meaningful self-government, public accountability, and responsible citizenship might look like when ordinary people remember that authority ultimately belongs to them.</p>
<p>And then there's <strong>Feed the Puppet</strong>, which began in the territory of meditation, spiritual exploration, and conscious living before developing a rather alarming population of irreverent characters, questionable spiritual authorities, and creatures whose relationship with conventional wisdom is, at best, strained.</p>
<p>Beneath the absurdity, however, the questions are serious: How do we live more consciously? How do we recognize our own illusions? And is enlightenment still enlightenment if a judgmental pigeon shits on it?</p>
<p>These projects may look like distant relatives who should never be seated together at Thanksgiving.</p>
<p>But they're all expressions of the same underlying curiosity: what makes people tick, what makes systems fail, and what might happen if we become a little more honest about both.</p>
